## Scenario

```java
String a1 = "ABC";
String a2 = "ABC";
String a3 = new String("ABC");

HashMap<String,Integer> hm = new HashMap<>();
hm.put(a1,2);
hm.put(a2,1);
hm.put(a3,3);

System.out.println(hm.size());
```

## Output

```text
{ABC=3}
size = 1
```

## Explanation

All three String objects contain the same value 'ABC'.

String overrides both equals() and hashCode().

When HashMap inserts a2 and a3, it finds an existing key because:

- hashCode() is the same.
- equals() returns true.

As a result, the value is updated instead of creating a new entry.

## Interview Checkpoint

HashMap uses hashCode() to find the bucket and equals() to identify the exact key. Equal String values always map to the same logical key.

## Rule

A subclass cannot reduce the visibility of an overridden method.

## Examples

- public → public ✅
- protected → protected or public ✅
- default → default, protected, or public ✅
- private → cannot be overridden

## Invalid Example

```java
class Parent {
    public void display() {}
}

class Child extends Parent {
    protected void display() {}
}
```

This results in a compilation error because visibility is reduced.

## Interview Checkpoint

Overriding methods can maintain or increase visibility, but never decrease it.

## Can Parent and Child Both Have main()?

Yes, both parent and child classes can define their own main() methods.

```java
class Parent {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Parent Main");
    }
}

class Child extends Parent {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Child Main");
    }
}
```

## Why?

main() is static and static methods are not overridden.

This is called method hiding rather than method overriding.

## Interview Checkpoint

Static methods belong to the class rather than the object, so main() methods in parent and child classes are independent of each other.

## Can Abstract Classes Have Constructors?

Yes.

Although abstract classes cannot be instantiated directly, their constructors are executed when child classes are instantiated.

## Why?

Constructors initialize inherited fields and common state.

## Example

```java
abstract class Vehicle {

    Vehicle() {
        System.out.println("Vehicle Constructor");
    }
}

class Car extends Vehicle {
}
```

Creating a Car object automatically executes the Vehicle constructor.

## Interview Checkpoint

Constructors in abstract classes help initialize common properties shared across subclasses.
